nodecode-usage-guard is a rewrite of mister-ideas's mister-ideas/usage-guard:

  https://github.com/mister-ideas/usage-guard
  commit d59f798e848f5310f9e3d8673c01f383207c695e
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 mister-ideas (LICENSE)

What was rewritten. The whole guard, in Common Lisp against the Nodecode cell
contract: the two usage sources (the Claude OAuth usage endpoint and the Codex
usage endpoint), read with each app's own sign-in, the per-source reading floor
and freshness window, the cache that keeps the last good windows through a
failed or rate-limited read for a grace period, the fail-open policy, the
warning levels, the `/usage-guard` status surface, and the reading of a
Codex rate-limit answer. The original's settings became the cell's section:
threshold_percent, warn_percents, max_age_s, min_interval_s, stale_grace_s
and fail_open keep their names and defaults; the per-source intervals of the
original (180s for both subscription sources) are the default floor.

What was left out, and why. The original reads usage through its host's own
fetcher and refuses the request in the host's llm_execution middleware; a
Nodecode cell has no pre-call veto, so the refusal is the verdict line the
tool, the command and the round note carry, and the request is never
blocked. The MoA preset resolution, the near-wall output-budget cap, the
borrowed snapshots (hydrate_from_paths) and the desktop chip have no
counterpart here: Nodecode has no MoA presets, caps its own request budgets,
has no borrowed snapshot files, and its surfaces are the tool and the command.
